export const KNOWLEDGE_DOCUMENT = "<portfolio>\n<doc id=\"profile\" title=\"Profile\">\nJeevesh Bodhani: AI / ML & Computer Vision Engineer (Artificial Intelligence & Data Science Engineer).\nB.Tech in Artificial Intelligence and Data Science (2024 - 2028) at Dwarkadas J. Sanghvi College of Engineering, 9.6 Cumulative CGPA. Based in Mumbai, Maharashtra, India.\nFocus areas: real-time computer vision pipelines, deep-learning model fine-tuning and evaluation, quantitative financial modelling, and production full-stack systems (FastAPI backends, React dashboards).\nBuilt projects at several 2026 hackathons, including the Drishti AI Hackathon, Smart India Hackathon, PCCOE Craftverse and the Decentralised Intelligence Hackathon.\n</doc>\n<doc id=\"skills\" title=\"Technical skills\">\nLanguages: Python, C, JavaScript / TypeScript, SQL, C++ basics.\nAI / ML: PyTorch, OpenCV, Computer Vision, Object Detection (YOLO / Custom), Pose Estimation, Deep Learning, Model Fine-Tuning & Evaluation, Image Segmentation.\nData & systems: FastAPI, React, NumPy, Pandas, Time-Series Forecasting, Data Visualization, REST APIs, Git & GitHub.\nFinance & quant: Financial Modeling (DCF), Credit Risk Analysis, Company & Market Valuation, Quantitative Telemetry Modeling.\nOther strengths: Structured Problem-Solving, Camera-Calibrated Spatial Mapping, Statistical Baseline Calibration, Rapid Prototyping under Hackathon Deadlines.\n</doc>\n<doc id=\"contact\" title=\"Contact & links\">\nEmail: jeeveshbodhani5@gmail.com\nGitHub: https://github.com/vizsh\nLinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/in/jeeveshbodhani21\nResume: a PDF is available on this site (Resume.pdf on the desktop, or the Recruiter Summary page at /recruiter).\nThe Showcase Explorer also has a contact form (\"Contact Transmission\").\n</doc>\n<doc id=\"jarvis-hedge-fund\" title=\"JARVIS — Autonomous Quantitative Hedge Fund\">\nJARVIS — Autonomous Quantitative Hedge Fund (Full-Stack, 2026).\nAn algorithmic hedge fund and portfolio management engine built on a strict zero-hallucination paradigm: “no model writes a figure” — every financial calculation is deterministic code you can audit.\nTech: Python, Quantitative Modeling, DCF, FastAPI, React, PyTest (532 tests), NLP.\nRepository: https://github.com/vizsh/Jarvis_Hedge_Fund\nGitHub activity (snapshot of 12 Nov 2026): 72 commits on master; last commit on 6 Oct 2026 (\"Rewrite README with diagrams and prototype screenshots; add business, impact, competitor and pilot docs; fix home tile l\").\nHighlights listed on the site:\n- 532 passing automated tests with CI verification badge and 72 deep iteration commits\n- Deterministic financial verification: every valuation, DCF model, and sensitivity check runs audited arithmetic rather than generative guesswork\n- Bilingual conversational interface supporting seamless financial queries in both English and Hindi\n- Quantitative portfolio rebalancing optimizing Sharpe ratio and mitigating downside risk via automated stop-losses\nCase study (written from the project's own repository):\nSummary: A bilingual (English/Hindi) money assistant where no language model ever writes a number: every figure comes from deterministic, tested code.\nProblem: Three things cost Indian households money: digital fraud (about ₹22,845 crore lost in 2024, I4C as reported), informal credit (a moneylender quoting ₹5 per ₹100 a month is 60% a year on simple interest), and fees and unverified tips. Each has a moment where a short, plain check would change the outcome, yet most tools either hide the maths or let a language model invent figures.\nConstraints:\n- Constraint: The model never writes a number: figures are computed by code and placed into templates.\n- Constraint: The risk engine never imports a model.\n- Constraint: A translation may never change a figure: English and Hindi answers share the same computed values.\n- Constraint: Market data is a frozen public snapshot built into SQLite; the local model (Ollama) is optional.\nArchitecture: Question (Typed or spoken, English or Hindi) -> Understand (Whole-sentence intent parsing (understand.py)) -> Rule router (Rules first; an optional local model may only pick one intent) -> Pure calculator (Deterministic analysis, rural-credit and risk maths) -> Template (Hand-written Hindi/English text; numbers placed by code) -> Answer card (Figures, charts and next steps)\nKey decisions:\n- Decision: Three layers: truth, meaning, words, because Numbers live in pure modules, intent in rules, and wording in templates, so a wording change can never alter a figure.\n- Decision: Optional local model, never required, because Without Ollama the assistant asks a clarifying question instead of guessing.\n- Decision: Hash-chained audit record with a tamper test, because Trade-like actions leave an evidence trail that shows if it was edited.\nResults:\n- Result: 532, pytest tests (python -m pytest tests/)\n- Result: 10, features across fraud, credit and investing protection\n- Result: 32, checked explanations in English and Hindi\n- Result: 19, government schemes in the rural tools\nHonest limits:\n- Limit: Business numbers are assumptions: no pilot has run and no bank has used the product.\n- Limit: WhatsApp delivery is a simulator; going live needs a Twilio account and approved templates.\n- Limit: Prices are a frozen snapshot covering a 53-company universe, not live data.\n- Limit: The tip scanner reads English tips only, and Hindi speech recognition is untested with rural speakers.\n- Limit: The audit chain is tamper-evident, not tamper-proof: anchoring outside the machine is not built.\n- Limit: No research desk beats a coin flip, and the project says so and builds around it.\nNext steps:\n- Next: Run a pilot with a real partner to replace assumed business numbers.\n- Next: Ship live WhatsApp delivery with approved templates.\n- Next: Anchor the audit chain outside the machine.\n- Next: Test Hindi speech recognition with rural speakers.\nA live in-browser demo of this project is available on the site's desktop.\n</doc>\n<doc id=\"drishti-ai\" title=\"Drishti AI (Kinesis) — Real-Time Exam Behavioral Surveillance\">\nDrishti AI (Kinesis) — Real-Time Exam Behavioral Surveillance (AI & Systems, 2026).\nA privacy-first real-time computer vision pipeline monitoring exam-hall behaviour from standard CCTV footage without facial biometrics. Resolves identity via camera-calibrated seat positioning instead of biometric databases.\nTech: Python, PyTorch, OpenCV, TensorRT, FastAPI, React, Pose Tracking.\nRepository: https://github.com/vizsh/Drishti_AI\nGitHub activity (snapshot of 12 Nov 2026): 79 commits on master; last commit on 23 Aug 2026 (\"Recalibrate Hall D from measured pixel grid — 3 confident seats, not 6 guessed\").\nHighlights listed on the site:\n- Highest commit iteration (79 commits) with a modular layered architecture across perception, behaviour, and calibration\n- Fine-tuned custom object-detection model on 25,000+ images achieving 92% mAP50, beating the benchmark baseline\n- Statistical baseline calibration scoring each student against individual norms, eliminating rigid alert cascades\n- 8 staged live demonstration scripts and full docs/ suite for turnkey audit deployment\nCase study (written from the project's own repository):\nSummary: Real-time exam-hall monitoring from CCTV that never uses face recognition and scores each student against their own baseline to cut false positives.\nProblem: CCTV proctoring fails in two ways: privacy (face recognition to identify students) and false positives. The problem statement's own risk #1 is normal behaviour such as thinking, stretching or looking around being flagged as misconduct. Every design decision targets that failure mode without losing recall on real misconduct.\nConstraints:\n- Constraint: No face recognition, ever: identity is the seat, anchored by homography, never a face embedding.\n- Constraint: Raw video never leaves the edge process; only keypoints, risk scores and face-blurred evidence clips do.\n- Constraint: Every alert carries a deterministic, template-based explanation from measured features, never a bare confidence score.\n- Constraint: Score each student against their own calibrated baseline (z-score), not a global threshold.\n- Constraint: Production target: NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano at the edge.\nArchitecture: Ingest (RTSP/ONVIF, hardware decode, ~10 fps sampling) -> Detect (YOLO11n person detection) -> Track (BoT-SORT multi-object tracking) -> Seat anchor (Homography maps each track to a seat: identity = seat) -> Pose + objects (YOLO11-pose (One-Euro filtered) + fine-tuned YOLO11 for phone / paper / earpiece) -> Baseline (Per-seat buffer, z-score vs. the student's own settling window) -> Behaviour (ST-GCN++ (PYSKL) fused with rule-based scoring) -> Explain (Risk fusion → FastAPI/WebSocket → React dashboard with template explanations)\nKey decisions:\n- Decision: YOLO11n over RTMDet-tiny, Faster-RCNN or DETR, because Direct TensorRT export for a low-cost edge box matters more than 1–2 mAP points; two-stage detectors are too slow for real time.\n- Decision: Seat as identity, because No face data is stored, and identity survives tracker ID switches.\n- Decision: Feedback loop on dismissed alerts, because Dismissing an alert widens that student's threshold live, so the same false positive doesn't repeat.\nResults:\n- Result: 8, staged demos (run_stage1…8_demo.py), each stage tested in isolation\n- Result: 5, modules: ingestion, calibration, perception, behaviour, risk engine\n- Result: 91.7%, benchmark target: accuracy of a published pose + YOLO system used as the bar to beat\nHonest limits:\n- Limit: Earpieces are hard to detect at CCTV distance, and the docs say so rather than overclaiming.\n- Limit: Public cheating datasets are close-up and well-lit and do not match an overhead CCTV view, so own staged footage is required.\n- Limit: Seat calibration needs 4+ known floor points per camera; the latest commit recalibrated a hall to 3 confident seats instead of 6 guessed ones.\nNext steps:\n- Next: Deploy on the Jetson Orin Nano edge target, the final stage of the build order.\n- Next: Grow the own-footage dataset for phone, paper and earpiece classes.\nA live in-browser demo of this project is available on the site's desktop.\n</doc>\n<doc id=\"neuroscope-3d\" title=\"NeuroScope 3D — Decentralised Cranial Tumor Segmentation\">\nNeuroScope 3D — Decentralised Cranial Tumor Segmentation (3D & Graphics, 2026).\nAn in-browser medical imaging and cranial tumor volumetric segmentation viewer running real MONAI deep-learning models with verified Dice and HD95 accuracy on held-out patient data.\nTech: Three.js, WebGL, MONAI, PyTorch, DICOM Raymarching, React, PyTest (48 tests).\nRepository: https://github.com/vizsh/DecentralisedIntelligence_NeuroScope3d_Outliers3797_PS02\nGitHub activity (snapshot of 12 Nov 2026): 42 commits on main; last commit on 17 Oct 2026 (\"Refresh README/FEATURES for judge review: current screenshots, verified new features\").\nHighlights listed on the site:\n- 17/17 judge tests passing with 48 automated test suites verifying sub-millimeter anatomical clipping\n- Real MONAI volumetric segmentation engine (zero mocked data) with measured Dice/HD95 metrics\n- Documented “no claim ships without a reproducible run” engineering discipline\n- Interactive 3D multi-planar raymarching across axial, coronal, and sagittal DICOM slices at 60 FPS\nCase study (written from the project's own repository):\nSummary: See a brain tumour in 3D, measure it exactly, and know how far to trust it: real MONAI segmentation with a measured uncertainty signal, fully local.\nProblem: A brain MRI study for suspected glioma is four 3D sequences of about 155 slices each, roughly 620 flat images per case. Understanding the tumour's 3D shape means reconstructing it mentally, and a confident-looking AI overlay on one slice doesn't say whether the 3D prediction underneath can be trusted. The thesis: a 3D viewer alone isn't new; a 3D viewer that tells you whether to trust what it shows is.\nConstraints:\n- Constraint: Research and teaching prototype only: never a diagnosis, never a substitute for a radiologist.\n- Constraint: Fully local, 0 cloud calls: uploads are deleted after processing and derived artefacts expire in 23 h.\n- Constraint: A 120 s per-case budget (T4 GPU reference).\n- Constraint: Fail-safe validation: a partially valid study produces zero output, never a degraded one.\nArchitecture: Ingest (Multi-sequence MRI and DICOM, grouped by series UID, de-identification checked) -> Validate (Geometry, orientation, affine agreement, finite values, skull-strip heuristics) -> Segment (MONAI SegResNet sliding-window inference, sha256-pinned checkpoint) -> Uncertainty (Flip-TTA ensemble → measured disagreement heat-map) -> Mesh (Surface Nets extraction of nested WT / TC / ET isosurfaces) -> Review (Linked axial / coronal / sagittal views, cutaway plane, volumes with formulas)\nKey decisions:\n- Decision: Measured uncertainty as the differentiator, because Flip-TTA disagreement is shown as a toggleable heat-map with an honest caveat wherever it appears.\n- Decision: Every volume shows its formula, because Voxel count × spacing on the original acquisition grid, so any number can be checked by hand.\n- Decision: HTTP Range requests into the 7.6 GB Decathlon archive, because Reads the tar's 512-byte headers and fetches only the needed cases: about 99 MB instead of 7.6 GB.\nResults:\n- Result: 17/17, judges' pipeline tests passing\n- Result: 0.88 / 0.87 / 0.79, Dice (WT / TC / ET) on 10 real held-out MSD cases\n- Result: 48, pytest tests\n- Result: 0, cloud calls: inference and copilot run locally\nHonest limits:\n- Limit: The uncertainty signal is real but modest: σ correlation 0.3463 pooled over 10 held-out cases (disagreement rises from 15.7% to about 65% as σ increases).\n- Limit: Accuracy is measured on 10 held-out cases: enough for the problem statement, not for clinical claims.\n- Limit: About 60–75 s per case on CPU only, so demos use a prewarmed cache of real runs.\nNext steps:\n- Next: Evaluate on more held-out cases before making any broader accuracy claim.\n- Next: Bring CPU inference closer to the 120 s budget without relying on the prewarmed cache.\nA live in-browser demo of this project is available on the site's desktop.\n</doc>\n<doc id=\"pillai-hotel360\" title=\"Smart Resort 360 (Hotel360) — Hospitality Decision Science\">\nSmart Resort 360 (Hotel360) — Hospitality Decision Science (Full-Stack, 2026).\nAn enterprise resort decision-science platform integrating real-time CCTV computer vision occupancy estimation with weather-aware demand simulation across 12 analytical modules.\nTech: Next.js, React, TypeScript, CCTV Computer Vision, Time-Series, CI/CD.\nRepository: https://github.com/vizsh/Pillai_Hotel360\nGitHub activity (snapshot of 12 Nov 2026): 56 commits on main; last commit on 27 Sept 2026 (\"Add the 16th Automation Scenario: social signal predicts a weather event\"); latest CI run (CI) passed.\nHighlights listed on the site:\n- 56 commits, 100MB production codebase with CI pipeline automated verification badge\n- Twelve predictive decision-science modules balancing guest check-in rates, room turnover, and utility loads\n- Real-time CCTV computer vision model monitoring pool, dining hall, and lobby spatial density\n- Weather-aware dynamic revenue simulation adjusting staff scheduling and supply ordering\nCase study (written from the project's own repository):\nSummary: One live, explainable digital twin of a resort: twelve decision-science modules, CCTV vision and weather-aware what-if simulation that all read from the same model.\nProblem: A resort runs on five departments that each keep their own record of the truth: front office on a spreadsheet, maintenance on a logbook, housekeeping on a walkie-talkie, revenue on a static rate card, guest requests on a phone call. None talk to each other in real time and none explain why a decision was made, so the property is permanently reacting.\nConstraints:\n- Constraint: Explainable by construction: every recommendation shows its measured basis (a Weibull hazard, an elasticity delta), never a bare confidence score.\n- Constraint: Numbers come from code: any figure ≥ 1,000 in an AI reply must appear in the data the model was given (±0.6%).\n- Constraint: Every value is tagged SIMULATED, MODELED or DERIVED.\n- Constraint: Graceful degradation: a blocked API or missing key degrades one feature, never the app.\nArchitecture: Signals (Live weather, 7 public feeds, CCTV test clips) -> Digital twin (Seeded deterministic simulation of the property) -> 12 modules (Weibull maintenance, elasticity pricing, Holt/EOQ inventory, k-means segmentation…) -> What-if (Monte Carlo on the real tick engine with online Bayesian calibration) -> Assistants (Ops + Concierge with automatic Ollama fallback and a grounding gate) -> Act (Accepting a recommendation dispatches a visible consequence on the twin)\nKey decisions:\n- Decision: Persistence-gated everywhere a signal is noisy, because One spike never becomes an alert, for maintenance, sentiment and CCTV alike.\n- Decision: Autopilot calls the same accept() as a human click, because Automation has a visible off-ramp and can be stopped at any time.\nResults:\n- Result: 270+, tests across modules, simulation engine, auth/RBAC and the grounding gate\n- Result: 12, decision-science modules sharing one model\n- Result: 15, narrated automation scenarios\nHonest limits:\n- Limit: Sentiment is lexicon-based, and pricing blends elasticity by guest mix rather than estimating per channel.\n- Limit: CCTV fire and altercation detectors are calibrated heuristics, not trained classifiers.\n- Limit: The property, guests, staff and telemetry are simulated; weather and map are fixed to Goa as a stand-in property.\n- Limit: The Nugen training service returned HTTP 502 at the time, so the assistants run on the Ollama fallback.\nNext steps:\n- Next: Replace heuristic CCTV detectors with trained classifiers.\n- Next: Estimate price elasticity per channel.\n</doc>\n<doc id=\"sih-ibvap\" title=\"SIH IBVAP — Integrated Border Vehicle & Person Surveillance\">\nSIH IBVAP — Integrated Border Vehicle & Person Surveillance (AI & Systems, 2026).\nA surveillance system engineered against an official Government of India problem statement (Ministry of Home Affairs / SSB, Smart India Hackathon). Features cross-post vehicle-person correlation and automatic number plate recognition.\nTech: Python, OpenCV, PyTorch, ANPR, Vehicle Re-ID, Multi-Camera Tracking.\nRepository: https://github.com/vizsh/SIH_IBVAP\nGitHub activity (snapshot of 12 Nov 2026): 35 commits on main; last commit on 29 Sept 2026 (\"Fix broken demo on fresh clone: check in videos + model weights, add one-click demo login\").\nHighlights listed on the site:\n- Built specifically against Ministry of Home Affairs / Sashastra Seema Bal (SSB) specifications\n- Automatic Number Plate Recognition (ANPR) optimized for varied plate fonts, angles, and dirt occlusion\n- Cross-post correlation tracking suspect vehicles moving between distributed checkpoints\n- Multi-camera person re-identification (Re-ID) mapping occupants entering and exiting transport\nCase study (written from the project's own repository):\nSummary: A software fusion layer that turns SSB's existing border CCTV into an AI surveillance platform without buying a single new camera.\nProblem: Problem statement 26187 (Ministry of Home Affairs, Sashastra Seema Bal): border outposts already have CCTV, but footage is watched rather than analysed. The ask is detection, tracking, virtual fencing, ANPR, pose and thermal analysis and cross-post correlation on the existing camera network.\nConstraints:\n- Constraint: Software only, on top of cameras that are already installed.\n- Constraint: Honesty rule: every number on screen is computed from real pipeline output or clearly labelled as an assumption or simulation.\n- Constraint: Tamper-evident audit log for every security event.\nArchitecture: Edge cameras (Four camera processes running simultaneously on real footage) -> Detect + track (YOLOv8n + ByteTrack, YOLOv8n-pose skeletons) -> Rules (Virtual-fence intrusion and loitering) -> ANPR (Multi-frame fusion with EasyOCR) -> Thermal (Thermal-trained detector for a dedicated LWIR sentry camera) -> Backend (FastAPI, confidence-tiered routing, hash-chained audit log) -> Correlate (Cross-BOP matching with haversine travel-time prediction) -> Console (React command console with a 3D MapLibre + deck.gl map)\nKey decisions:\n- Decision: Thermal as a dedicated LWIR post, because No camera has co-located sensors, so it's never presented as an RGB camera switching to thermal.\n- Decision: Multi-frame ANPR fusion, because Combines OCR reads across frames instead of trusting a single frame.\n- Decision: Per-camera cooldown on confidence-tiered events, because Stops one noisy camera from flooding the operator.\nResults:\n- Result: 4, live camera pipelines running at once\n- Result: 7, console pages, from live feeds to the audit log\nHonest limits:\n- Limit: The multi-sensor oscilloscope is simulated (no UGS or microphone hardware is installed), and it carries a badge saying so.\n- Limit: The District Risk Index's terrain factor is a documented, geography-informed assumption, not a measured reading.\n- Limit: Checkpoint digital twins are schematic, not as-built scans.\nNext steps:\n- Next: Integrate real ground sensors in place of the simulated oscilloscope feed.\n</doc>\n<doc id=\"retro-3d-portfolio\" title=\"Retro 3D Desktop Portfolio & Windows 98 OS\">\nRetro 3D Desktop Portfolio & Windows 98 OS (3D & Graphics, 2026).\nAn interactive 3D computing workstation merging physical desk exploration with a pixel-perfect, draggable Windows 98 operating system featuring games, terminal, document viewer, and audio synthesis.\nTech: React 19, TypeScript, Three.js, React Three Fiber, GLSL, Web Audio API.\nRepository: https://github.com/vizsh/updated_portfolio_website\nGitHub activity (snapshot of 12 Nov 2026): 4 commits on main; last commit on 19 Oct 2026 (\"chore(vercel): configure byte-range streaming for demo video and refine SPA rewrites\").\nHighlights listed on the site:\n- Physical 3D desk with interactive day/night study lamp, procedural coffee steam, and CRT bloom\n- Authentic Windows 98 desktop with wireframe ghost drag outlines, start menu, and taskbar\n- Playable Snake.exe, Minesweeper, MS-DOS Prompt with interactive commands\n- Zero-latency Web Audio API mechanical sound synthesis on every interaction\nCase study (written from the project's own repository):\nSummary: This site: a 3D room and a working Windows 98 desktop, with live GitHub numbers, in-browser project demos and evidence-backed case studies.\nProblem: Most portfolios are a wall of claims that a reviewer cannot check. This one is built to be explored, and to be verifiable: repo numbers come from GitHub at build time, demos run in your browser, and every case study links to its sources.\nConstraints:\n- Constraint: Must stay fast for recruiters: the one-page summary loads without the 3D engine.\n- Constraint: Static Vite build on Vercel, plus one optional serverless function for the chat.\n- Constraint: Demos process camera frames in the browser; nothing is uploaded.\nArchitecture: Build (GitHub API snapshot + per-project share pages, generated at build time) -> 3D room (React Three Fiber scene with baked GLB models, lazy-loaded) -> Win98 OS (Window manager, taskbar, start menu, deep-linkable apps) -> Apps (Showcase, Terminal, live demos, Ask Jeevesh chat)\nKey decisions:\n- Decision: Code-split three.js out of the first load, because The recruiter view and deep links render before the 1 MB 3D bundle arrives.\n- Decision: Build-time GitHub snapshot instead of client-side API calls, because Visitors never hit GitHub's rate limits, and numbers still refresh on every deploy.\nResults:\n- Result: 6, projects with shareable deep links\n- Result: 3, in-browser demos: JARVIS, Drishti and NeuroScope\nHonest limits:\n- Limit: Link previews have per-project titles and descriptions, but no per-project images yet.\n- Limit: The chat needs an API key on the server; without one it falls back to local keyword search.\nNext steps:\n- Next: Generate per-project preview images.\n- Next: Add privacy-friendly analytics on which windows people open.\n</doc>\n<doc id=\"demos\" title=\"Live demos on this site\">\nThe site has three in-browser demos, each a window on the retro desktop:\n- JARVIS.exe (/apps/jarvis): deterministic finance calculators (DCF valuation with a sensitivity table, a moneylender interest check, and a fee-drag calculator), showing every step of the maths.\n- Drishti.exe (/apps/drishti): webcam or video behaviour monitoring with public MediaPipe models; pixelates faces, learns a per-person baseline and raises persistence-gated, explained alerts. Frames never leave the browser.\n- NeuroScope3D.exe (/apps/neuroscope): a synthetic brain-tumour phantom with nested WT/TC/ET regions, a cutaway plane, a linked slice view and volumes that show their formula. Not patient data.\nIn the Ask Jeevesh chat, visitors can type commands such as \"open jarvis\", \"show the Drishti case study\", \"open the IBVAP repo\", \"open resume\" or \"open recruiter summary\", or type \"help\".\n</doc>\n<doc id=\"experience\" title=\"Experience (job simulations)\">\nJPMorgan Chase & Co.: Investment Banking Job Simulation (2026, Quantitative Simulation).\n- Identified optimal M&A target options for a corporate client based on strict strategic and quantitative financial evaluation criteria.\n- Constructed and calibrated a Discounted Cash Flow (DCF) model to compute enterprise and equity valuations.\n- Stress-tested valuation models against sudden competitor bids and severe supply chain disruptions.\nSkills: Financial Modeling (DCF), Valuation Analysis, M&A Assessment, Sensitivity Analysis.\n\nGoldman Sachs: Risk Management Job Simulation (2026, Quantitative Simulation).\n- Conducted comprehensive credit risk and quantitative analysis utilizing detailed financial datasets to evaluate personal finance implications.\n- Applied structured risk modeling to complex commercial real estate scenarios to assess operational and default probabilities.\nSkills: Credit Risk Modeling, Quantitative Analysis, Default Stress Testing, Risk Evaluation.\n</doc>\n<doc id=\"hackathons\" title=\"Hackathon builds (2026)\">\n- Drishti AI Hackathon: Kinesis AI — Exam Behavioral Surveillance. Engineered a real-time computer vision pipeline monitoring exam-hall behaviour from standard CCTV footage without collecting facial biometrics, resolving identity via camera-calibrated seat positioning. (Python, PyTorch, OpenCV, TensorRT, FastAPI)\n- PCCOE Craftverse: JARVIS — Algorithmic Hedge Fund Engine. Built an auditable financial computation tool with bilingual English/Hindi interface and 532 passing automated tests, adhering to a strict zero-hallucination deterministic calculation rule. (Python, FastAPI, PyTest (532 tests), DCF, React)\n- Smart India Hackathon: SIH IBVAP — Border Vehicle & Person Surveillance (MHA / SSB). Engineered automatic number plate recognition (ANPR) and cross-post vehicle-person correlation algorithms addressing a national border security problem statement. (Python, OpenCV, ANPR, Vehicle Re-ID, PyTorch)\n- Decentralised Intelligence Hackathon: NeuroScope 3D — Cranial Tumor Segmentation. Developed an in-browser 3D raymarching DICOM viewer powered by validated MONAI convolutional models, passing 17/17 judge verification tests. (Three.js, MONAI, PyTorch, WebGL, PyTest)\n- Sathaye College Hackathon: Zenith — Rapid Engineering Prototype. Delivered an end-to-end software solution under timed team hackathon constraints. (Python, React, REST APIs, FastAPI)\n- PCCOE Hacktopia & NMFC TechTatva: Industrial Telemetry & Medical X-Ray Diagnostics. Developed machine learning prototypes for automated fault detection in spatial telemetry and medical X-ray datasets. (PyTorch, OpenCV, Scikit-Learn, NumPy)\n</doc>\n<doc id=\"education\" title=\"Education\">\nB.Tech in Artificial Intelligence and Data Science, Dwarkadas J. Sanghvi College of Engineering (DJSCE) (Sep 2024 – May 2028, Mumbai, Maharashtra): Cumulative CGPA: 9.6.\n- Maintaining consistent academic standing with 9.6 CGPA in AI & Data Science coursework.\n- Active participation in technical hackathons, systems engineering, and quantitative research.\nHSC Higher Secondary Certificate Board Examination, Pace Junior Science College (May 2022 – Feb 2024, Mumbai, Maharashtra): Aggregate: 86.67%.\n- Rigorous foundation in advanced physics, chemistry, and higher mathematics.\nICSE Indian Certificate of Secondary Education, Gundecha Education Academy (Completed May 2022, Mumbai, Maharashtra): Aggregate: 94.1% (Mathematics: 99/100).\n- Distinction in Mathematics (99/100) and Science.\n</doc>\n<doc id=\"certifications\" title=\"Certifications\">\n- Using Machine Learning in Trading and Finance, New York Institute of Finance & Google Cloud (Apr 2026): Grade: 100%\n- Introduction to Trading, Machine Learning & GCP, Google Cloud & New York Institute of Finance (Mar 2026): Grade: 99%\n- Introduction to Tech Entrepreneurship, IIT Bombay (Jan 2026): Grade: 93%\n- Introduction to Front-End Development, Meta (2025): Grade: 92%\n- Generative AI Foundations Certificate, Microsoft (2025): Certified\n</doc>\n</portfolio>";
